  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "If \\(x,\\) \\(y\\) and \\(2x+\\frac y2\\) are not zero, then \\[ \\left(2x+\\frac y2\\right)^{-1}\\left[(2x)^{-1}+\\left(\\frac y2\\right)^{-1}\\right] \\] equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(xy^{-1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^{-1}y\\)",
    "D": "\\((xy)^{-1}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The exponent \\(-1\\) means reciprocal. Put \\(u=2x\\) and \\(v=y/2\\). The given conditions ensure that \\(u\\), \\(v\\), and \\(u+v\\) are nonzero, and \\(uv=xy\\).\n\nAdding the reciprocals produces \\(u+v\\) in the numerator, which cancels the outside denominator:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{u+v}\\left(\\frac1u+\\frac1v\\right)\n&=\\frac1{u+v}\\cdot\\frac{u+v}{uv}\\\\\n&=\\frac1{uv}\\\\\n&=\\frac1{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(u+v\\ne0\\). Thus the expression equals \\((xy)^{-1}\\), choice D.",
